{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778077284,"nonce":"PnO8bUMOR7YQoS0fMpxZ9aoYk3A=","title":"auth_result inbox payload conflates \"completed\" with \"succeeded\" — agents misread failed merges as successful","message":"## Symptom\n\nThe `UserPromptSubmit` hook surfaces auth results to agents as a system-reminder of the form:\n\n```\n[mandible-inbox] auth_result \u003cid\u003e — branch: \u003cbranch\u003e — status: completed\n```\n\nThe word \"completed\" is the value of the `status` field on the auth result. But `status: \"completed\"` only means \"the merge command finished executing\"; it says nothing about whether the merge actually succeeded. A failed merge with `exit_code: 1` carries the same `status: \"completed\"`.\n\nAs a result, the agent reads the system-reminder, interprets \"completed\" as \"merge succeeded,\" and proceeds (or reports to the user) as if the protected-branch merge went through.\n\n## Concrete repro\n\nAuth request `6ce9e43e-2d58-47fe-a3e5-5857a851a369`, branch `feature/synthesis-review`:\n\n- `GET /api/inbox` returned `{\"status\":\"completed\",\"exit_code\":1,\"error\":null,...}`\n- `GET /api/auth/request/6ce9e43e` returned the same: `status: \"completed\"`, `exit_code: 1`, with `output` containing `\"Working tree is dirty ... Clean up your working tree before merging.\"`\n- The `UserPromptSubmit` hook surfaced only `status: completed`.\n\nThe agent in this session read that as a successful merge and told the user the merge had gone through, when in fact it had failed.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`status` in the auth_result payload is the *workflow lifecycle* state (queued → running → completed). It does not encode success/failure of the underlying command — that lives in `exit_code` (and `error`). The inbox/hook layer surfaces `status` but not `exit_code`, so consumers cannot distinguish success from failure without an extra API call.\n\n## Suggested fixes (any one or a combination)\n\n1. **Hook-side fix:** the `UserPromptSubmit` hook should include `exit_code` (and a derived `outcome: succeeded|failed` field) in the surfaced text. e.g. `[mandible-inbox] auth_result \u003cid\u003e — branch: \u003cb\u003e — outcome: failed (exit_code=1)`.\n2. **API-side fix:** add a top-level `outcome` field to the auth_result payload, distinct from lifecycle `status`. `outcome ∈ {succeeded, failed, cancelled}` once `status == completed`.\n3. **Naming fix:** rename the lifecycle field from `status` to `lifecycle` (or similar) to remove the ambiguity, and reserve `status` for the outcome.\n\nOption 1 is the smallest change and fixes the immediate misreading. Option 2 is the most robust for downstream consumers. Both are compatible.\n\n## Why this is important\n\nAgents act on auth results without polling — the hook output is the single signal. Misreading a failed merge as successful means agents will:\n- Tell the user the merge went through when it did not.\n- Skip remediation (cleaning the working tree, resubmitting, etc.).\n- Make further decisions on a false premise (e.g. \"main now has X, so I can branch from it\").\n\nThis is the same class of issue as the inbox-ack scoping bug (`b47891b`) — the inbox layer is a critical signalling channel and any ambiguity there propagates into wrong agent behavior.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A failed auth merge surfaces in the system-reminder in a way that an agent cannot reasonably interpret as success.\n- Existing successful-merge messages remain unambiguous.\n- The fix covers the inbox payload AND the `UserPromptSubmit` hook output (both must be unambiguous).","files":null}]}